1
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:04,400
Working with AI.

2
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:13,200
From Anthropic by Nathan Wilmers, Maxim Masenkoff, Peter McCrory, November 23rd, 2026.

3
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:16,320
Produced by Ask Who Casts AI.

4
00:00:21,454 --> 00:00:22,116
Summary.

5
00:00:23,406 --> 00:00:26,852
How are businesses across the economy adopting AI?

6
00:00:27,246 --> 00:00:30,084
What bottlenecks and challenges have they faced?

7
00:00:30,574 --> 00:00:36,196
How can employers reorganize human expertise around increasingly capable models?

8
00:00:37,198 --> 00:00:45,348
To understand businesses' experiences better, we conducted 66 interviews with 58 of Anthropic's enterprise customers.

9
00:00:46,126 --> 00:00:49,028
We found sources of AI value.

10
00:00:49,678 --> 00:00:54,308
Companies are deploying AI across a dizzying range of knowledge work tasks.

11
00:00:54,798 --> 00:01:01,828
In particular, they are increasingly using coding agents to bring software capabilities to non-technical roles.

12
00:01:02,638 --> 00:01:09,412
Interviewees report that most uses of AI add value by increasing their output rather than by cutting costs.

13
00:01:09,998 --> 00:01:17,572
They attribute this both to AI accelerating existing work and to its role in introducing novel product and service improvements.

14
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:19,684
Impact on employment.

15
00:01:20,238 --> 00:01:27,140
Across our interviews, we saw very few examples of domestic layoffs that were driven by AI automation.

16
00:01:27,854 --> 00:01:35,172
Although some companies reported hiring more due to AI, most companies reported hiring less due to AI.

17
00:01:35,886 --> 00:01:41,284
Many also reported reducing their use of contractors, particularly offshore ones.

18
00:01:41,646 --> 00:01:48,036
These reported cuts could be an early warning of job disruption in the international business services industry.

19
00:01:48,462 --> 00:01:49,280
Bottlenecks.

20
00:01:49,966 --> 00:01:59,076
Companies reported a range of bottlenecks, including experts resisting AI deployment and tasks that are not accelerated by AI.

21
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:08,740
As coding speeds up, for example, other tasks like code review, sales, and strategic decision making increasingly need to keep pace.

22
00:02:09,454 --> 00:02:12,644
Limits to top-down and bottom-up approaches.

23
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:20,644
Many companies approach AI by identifying a workflow to automate and rolling out a top-down solution.

24
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:31,280
This approach can lead to job insecurity, fears of workplace surveillance, and resistance from the experts needed to set up an effective and durable workflow.

25
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:43,940
Companies that distribute AI tools and wait for bottom-up adoption see some valuable AI use but face bottlenecks from unequal adoption and single-task AI implementations.

26
00:02:44,558 --> 00:02:46,404
Reorganizing for AI.

27
00:02:47,086 --> 00:02:52,900
Interviewees describe the highest value uses as involving meaningful reorganization.

28
00:02:53,710 --> 00:03:06,560
Some companies are broadening their employees' roles to ensure individuals can take ownership over whole workflows and are redeploying employees to the bottleneck tasks that AI models cannot do.

29
00:03:06,830 --> 00:03:16,548
Some also seek to empower employees by building out infrastructure to support non developer coding and by providing broad based internal access to business data.

30
00:03:17,262 --> 00:03:28,580
These companies are reorganizing to take advantage of ongoing model capability improvements rather than settling on a fixed approach that assumes AI tools will remain as powerful as they are today.

31
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:35,300
Introduction The use of LLMs has exploded across the economy.

32
00:03:35,886 --> 00:03:40,484
How are human organizations reshaping around these strange new collaborators?

33
00:03:41,358 --> 00:03:44,932
Consider an employee at a mid-size law firm that we spoke to.

34
00:03:45,134 --> 00:03:48,804
For years, the firm's mailroom had followed the same protocol.

35
00:03:49,262 --> 00:03:53,284
A scanner sent PDFs of incoming correspondence to an inbox.

36
00:03:53,710 --> 00:04:01,828
A mail clerk opened each PDF, skimmed it, clicked Save As, renamed it, and placed it in the firm's file system.

37
00:04:02,350 --> 00:04:04,644
The process took five minutes per letter.

38
00:04:05,390 --> 00:04:10,624
One day, a senior developer visited the mailroom to watch the mail being processed.

39
00:04:10,830 --> 00:04:22,019
In a month he built an AI system that eventually became two API calls, which named the document based on its first page and then assigned it a place in the firm's document system.

40
00:04:22,638 --> 00:04:24,516
The mail clerks were blown away.

41
00:04:24,942 --> 00:04:31,300
An employee at another law firm we interviewed hoped AI would create a second brain for the firm.

42
00:04:32,238 --> 00:04:35,780
For higher-level stuff, it just opens up possibilities.

43
00:04:36,558 --> 00:04:40,708
But the firm struggled to get everyone to use AI effectively.

44
00:04:41,358 --> 00:04:47,876
Demand letters to potential defendants were filled with excessive EM dashes and occasional errors.

45
00:04:48,750 --> 00:04:56,064
Attorneys building apps kept running into the wall based on hallucinated menus or buttons that don't exist.

46
00:04:56,910 --> 00:05:04,384
Others worried about confidentiality and data security, or they just didn't have time to get onto this AI train.

47
00:05:04,558 --> 00:05:13,668
A few power users inundated the firm with AI pros, casually sending Claude-prompted seven-page instructions to the IT team.

48
00:05:14,126 --> 00:05:19,780
A third law firm we spoke to adopted AI early and achieved strong uptake.

49
00:05:20,224 --> 00:05:27,428
One of the firm's early projects was an AI timekeeping system that saved attorneys' work tracking billable hours.

50
00:05:28,064 --> 00:05:38,784
IT staff saw parts of their job automated, but they then focused on building secure infrastructure to host apps and manage context that served the rest of the firm.

51
00:05:39,054 --> 00:05:46,692
As AI tools spread, the firm realized that associates were no longer needed to write first drafts of certain legal documents.

52
00:05:47,438 --> 00:05:52,784
But without writing, how does an associate know if that's a good draft or a bad draft?

53
00:05:53,102 --> 00:06:06,436
So it introduced a new workflow where junior lawyers were required to really take that first AI-written draft and work with a senior attorney, pairing off and marking up and editing the draft together.

54
00:06:06,830 --> 00:06:12,676
These three firms applied the same technology in the same industry, but in different ways.

55
00:06:13,486 --> 00:06:20,932
The first firm automated a workflow, creating efficiency gains but risking job displacement for the mail clerks.

56
00:06:21,742 --> 00:06:31,940
For the second, bottlenecks to adoption and effective implementation prevented job displacement, but also blunted productivity gains that would benefit the firm.

57
00:06:32,238 --> 00:06:41,348
The third reorganized work without eliminating any jobs, because effective use of the technology required redeploying employees toward new tasks.

58
00:06:42,126 --> 00:06:49,028
The firm redesigned roles to maintain human expertise as a complement to increasingly capable AI models.

59
00:06:49,614 --> 00:07:05,828
In this article, we explore the connections between AI-driven value and work reorganization through 66 face-to-face interviews conducted across 58 companies in the US, all of which are in the midst of adopting AI.

60
00:07:06,318 --> 00:07:10,852
We interviewed executives, managers, and individual contributors.

61
00:07:11,470 --> 00:07:16,676
We talked to employees at AI-native startups and 100-year-old businesses.

62
00:07:17,070 --> 00:07:20,420
We interviewed people at manufacturers and tech companies.

63
00:07:20,942 --> 00:07:30,276
We spoke with structural engineers, dentists and accountants, employees at hedge funds, law firms, and private equity firms.

64
00:07:30,958 --> 00:07:34,724
A property manager, a trucking company, and a publisher.

65
00:07:35,024 --> 00:07:40,100
They're all anthropic customers, but many also use other AI tools.

66
00:07:40,526 --> 00:07:51,012
They were selected for this study because of their range across industry, size, and AI usage, though they are not a random or systematically representative sample.

67
00:07:51,438 --> 00:08:01,988
As in other qualitative research, we present stories and a framework to organize them, with the expectation that follow up quantitative research can test these ideas further.

68
00:08:02,894 --> 00:08:07,524
The interviews were done anonymously, both for the interviewees and their companies.

69
00:08:08,046 --> 00:08:10,372
Any names in the report are pseudonyms.

70
00:08:11,982 --> 00:08:14,500
How are businesses adapting to AI?

71
00:08:14,990 --> 00:08:21,348
In our interviews, we gained insight into how AI has become embedded in businesses across the economy.

72
00:08:21,664 --> 00:08:29,540
Some of the AI agents we heard about are sending faxes back and forth as doctors' offices refer and intake patients.

73
00:08:30,144 --> 00:08:40,144
Other agents are giving advice to technicians when an X-ray machine breaks, and still others are then marking up the X-rays to help patients better understand them.

74
00:08:40,398 --> 00:08:46,564
Agents are reading property deeds from the 1800s and working out delivery routes for gaming machines.

75
00:08:47,502 --> 00:08:55,664
Recently, an AI agent analysed a company's organizational chart and suggested departments where the company could add headcount.

76
00:08:56,494 --> 00:09:00,772
Three heads here, we need to add two there, zero here.

77
00:09:01,504 --> 00:09:03,812
Mostly agents are writing code.

78
00:09:04,302 --> 00:09:11,844
Interviewees described 100 pieces of software that they wrote with AI in enough detail that we could categorize them.

79
00:09:12,590 --> 00:09:20,644
80% of this software automated previously manual work, like spreadsheet-based financial updates or taking meeting notes.

80
00:09:21,518 --> 00:09:27,396
Another 20% replaced existing software, often previously purchased from outside vendors.

81
00:09:27,726 --> 00:09:35,236
Agents are building apps for private parking enforcement, 401k audits, and trademark checks for new product names.

82
00:09:35,630 --> 00:09:42,244
They are making dashboards to track portfolio companies, equipment repairs, and debt collectors' phone calls.

83
00:09:42,638 --> 00:09:46,180
How have human organizations harnessed these new assistants?

84
00:09:47,024 --> 00:09:51,300
We use our interviews to explore several aspects of these deployments.

85
00:09:52,078 --> 00:10:02,596
We begin by estimating how much value interviewees ascribe to AI deployments and relate that to a measure of how much organizational change they required to implement.

86
00:10:03,470 --> 00:10:09,184
Figure 1 plots nearly 300 AI applications that interviewees discussed in detail.

87
00:10:09,390 --> 00:10:14,500
We define an application as a specific AI use case in a specific company.

88
00:10:14,990 --> 00:10:27,104
We coded discussions of each application according to the value interviewees attributed to it and the extent to which the organization had to make significant organizational changes to accommodate it.

89
00:10:27,406 --> 00:10:36,708
These value assessments are primarily qualitative descriptions on the part of interviewees, not typically based on A-B tests or other quantitative assessments.

90
00:10:37,646 --> 00:10:38,276
Footnote.

91
00:10:38,544 --> 00:10:46,372
Note that a given use case can be attributed different degrees of value and come with different levels of organizational change in different companies.

92
00:10:46,864 --> 00:10:55,716
Coding agents for front-end development, for example, can supercharge product development at one organization and degrade the code base at another.

93
00:10:56,740 --> 00:11:03,300
Image, A scatter chart titled High Value AI Use Cases Involve Organizational Change.

94
00:11:03,726 --> 00:11:15,424
Nearly 300 dots plot self-reported value from 0 to 100 against the extent of organizational change, from changing your own habits to fully merging multiple roles.

95
00:11:15,758 --> 00:11:20,996
A trend line rises to the right with a correlation of plus 0.39.

96
00:11:21,934 --> 00:11:22,704
Figure 1.

97
00:11:23,054 --> 00:11:25,508
AI use cases by organization.

98
00:11:25,998 --> 00:11:34,276
AI applications are arrayed by self-reported value to the business and the extent of organizational change interviewees reported.

99
00:11:35,310 --> 00:11:48,064
Self-reported value is coded on a scale from no value or net negative, convenience and small quality gains, material and measurable savings to a large economic effect on the company.

100
00:11:48,384 --> 00:12:00,452
Organizational change was coded by an automated reviewer on a scale of zero, only the user's own work changed, to 100, multiple roles or functions were merged or cut.

101
00:12:01,038 --> 00:12:11,044
The coder implemented this scale according to how deep a given change was, how many organizational changes were made, and how many different functions the change involved.

102
00:12:11,470 --> 00:12:21,572
Figure 1 shows that many light touch AI applications can fit into an existing organizational structure, like ad hoc data analysis or slide deck design.

103
00:12:22,064 --> 00:12:28,784
However, many of the simplest applications were rated as relatively low value by the employees who described them.

104
00:12:29,070 --> 00:12:44,836
Higher value deployments tend to require changes to processes, like AI review of every code commit, or roles, like a marketing manager who edits skill.md files containing branding guidelines instead of designing individual documents.

105
00:12:45,518 --> 00:12:57,732
Several interviewees described AI adoption as essentially a challenge of organizational change, flattening organizations, shifting reporting lines, or dropping functions.

106
00:12:58,862 --> 00:13:08,772
At one firm, for example, executives decided that junior analysts were no longer necessary because executives could complete their own SQL queries.

107
00:13:09,902 --> 00:13:11,872
As one accountant put it,

108
00:13:11,872 --> 00:13:16,484
it's a combination of process reengineering and systematic discipline.

109
00:13:17,294 --> 00:13:23,392
There are several reasons why the most valuable applications led to significant organizational change.

110
00:13:23,918 --> 00:13:37,508
In some cases, the AI applications required a structural change in order to be successful, such as when a policy had to be clarified so that AI agents could rigorously apply it in an assessment workflow.

111
00:13:37,742 --> 00:13:45,636
In other cases, initial success spurred excitement about making larger organizational changes to further harness AI.

112
00:13:46,062 --> 00:13:50,276
In others, the success of using AI forced downstream changes.

113
00:13:50,926 --> 00:13:56,612
One commercial real estate company saw its staff start sending out client-facing documents themselves.

114
00:13:56,942 --> 00:14:04,324
But this meant they had violated company rules that asked employees to route external materials through the marketing department.

115
00:14:04,672 --> 00:14:07,312
As an IT director explained,

116
00:14:07,312 --> 00:14:10,852
That's good because, hey, we got another client.

117
00:14:11,246 --> 00:14:16,068
That's bad because our standard operating procedures are starting to fall apart.

118
00:14:16,846 --> 00:14:26,148
These stories echo academic research that shows that organizational change is necessary to derive value from transformative technologies.

119
00:14:26,862 --> 00:14:31,748
Next, we studied what exactly value means in the context of AI adoption.

120
00:14:32,352 --> 00:14:41,796
Figure two shows that interviewees described most AI applications as adding value by increasing a firm's output rather than cutting costs.

121
00:14:42,512 --> 00:14:49,552
Around half of the applications that increased output were due to AI accelerating or expanding existing work.

122
00:14:49,774 --> 00:14:57,348
The other half were the result of AI improving the quality of products and services or creating new products altogether.

123
00:14:57,838 --> 00:15:06,756
For instance, a medical group improved service quality by using AI to give patients an upfront estimate of after insurance costs.

124
00:15:07,152 --> 00:15:13,252
A commercial real estate manager mocked up vacant units for potential tenants with their own branding.

125
00:15:14,030 --> 00:15:16,484
Here's what your sandwich place would look like in this space.

126
00:15:27,712 --> 00:15:28,032
A dental company introduced an after-hours AI answering service so patients' midnight calls no longer went to voicemail.

127
00:15:28,046 --> 00:15:34,308
Image A bar chart titled, Reported Value from AI Adoption comes primarily from business expansion.

128
00:15:34,734 --> 00:15:41,152
More output accounts for 55% of applications, led by increased quality at 14%.

129
00:15:42,158 --> 00:15:46,628
Costs cut account for 19%, mostly reduced hiring.

130
00:15:47,118 --> 00:15:52,272
Value kept by employees, 7%, and no value, 19%.

131
00:15:53,486 --> 00:15:54,404
Figure 2.

132
00:15:54,702 --> 00:15:59,872
How often different types of AI value come up in 66 interviews?

133
00:16:00,192 --> 00:16:08,868
AI applications, use cases by firm, in which an interviewee specified whether and where benefits of the application appeared.

134
00:16:09,326 --> 00:16:13,732
Bars reflect the percent of applications that produce different types of value.

135
00:16:14,254 --> 00:16:20,836
An automated coder classified applications according to the surrounding discussion in interview transcripts.

136
00:16:21,390 --> 00:16:32,292
In one tenth of the use cases laid out in Figure 2, value was captured directly by employees who were either doing more enjoyable work or were doing less work altogether.

137
00:16:32,910 --> 00:16:41,444
One manager described how bringing AI into his construction company meant that work can be a joy again instead of a difficult, painful thing.

138
00:16:41,742 --> 00:16:49,988
Senior engineers at an engineering consultancy described being thrilled to be free of tedious standards reconciliation work.

139
00:16:50,414 --> 00:17:04,387
This sentiment was particularly common in cases where AI sped up peripheral tasks that were distracting to experts, like making pitch decks for product developers or preparing CRM reports for sales representatives.

140
00:17:05,454 --> 00:17:17,892
An employee at one construction materials company said they appreciated a whole series of small, personal automations that were changing my life because now I do so much less work.

141
00:17:18,222 --> 00:17:25,700
An executive at a food industry company remarked on the number of people who just have thanked me because they could take a vacation.

142
00:17:26,606 --> 00:17:37,252
This is consistent with a Danish survey that found that around 10% of employees who save time with AI chatbots say they take more breaks or have more leisure time.

143
00:17:37,902 --> 00:17:40,224
But some managers were less enthused.

144
00:17:40,462 --> 00:17:47,664
One frustrated executive described his employees as hiding their AI-enabled successes, saying,

145
00:17:47,664 --> 00:17:52,772
They don't want you to know because they want to use those 10 hours a week to go walk their dog.

146
00:17:53,486 --> 00:18:02,404
A private equity manager suspected that among workers at companies in his portfolio, some of them are working basically half days.

147
00:18:02,926 --> 00:18:09,220
Our interviews show substantial variation in the degree and kind of value companies derive from AI.

148
00:18:09,998 --> 00:18:14,628
The highest value applications involve wide-reaching organizational change.

149
00:18:15,214 --> 00:18:20,132
These patterns warrant attention to the specifics of AI deployments across these companies.

150
00:18:20,494 --> 00:18:33,412
In the following section, we assess three common adoption strategies that we observed in our interviews: top-down automation, bottom-up experimentation, and a broader reorganization around AI.

151
00:18:34,734 --> 00:18:36,304
Top-down automation.

152
00:18:36,910 --> 00:18:38,304
Mark racked his brain.

153
00:18:38,670 --> 00:18:40,516
Had he messed anything up recently?

154
00:18:41,102 --> 00:18:50,404
As a paralegal at his law firm, he was rarely called into a partner's office, and even more rarely ended up in an office with two of the firm's equity partners.

155
00:18:51,310 --> 00:18:55,940
They wanted to talk about his AI use, a topic that filled him with dread.

156
00:18:56,686 --> 00:19:02,532
He had already realized that model's continued improvement would mean the beginning of the end for my work.

157
00:19:02,894 --> 00:19:03,556
He worried.

158
00:19:04,302 --> 00:19:06,820
Why would anyone want to employ someone like me?

159
00:19:07,566 --> 00:19:11,300
Mark's fears were understandable and far from unique.

160
00:19:11,822 --> 00:19:17,796
This past year, AI has loomed especially large in performance assessments across industries.

161
00:19:18,638 --> 00:19:28,304
Some of the most sobering moments in our interviews were when managers described making difficult decisions to terminate employees who wouldn't adopt AI.

162
00:19:28,544 --> 00:19:40,068
One engineering leader described experiencing a morale hit after the company terminated a developer who, they said, just kind of shut down instead of trying to embrace it.

163
00:19:40,686 --> 00:19:45,700
As one person at a financial services firm said of their company's AI push.

164
00:19:46,414 --> 00:19:48,484
You either get on board or don't.

165
00:19:48,846 --> 00:19:51,588
And this might not be the place for you if you don't.

166
00:19:52,366 --> 00:20:01,188
An engineering executive described grappling with the massive performance differences he saw emerging between AI users and AI resistors.

167
00:20:01,998 --> 00:20:03,784
Ultimately, he said,

168
00:20:03,784 --> 00:20:12,704
We had a couple people who started off extremely anti-AI, and they moved a little along the spectrum, but not enough.

169
00:20:13,070 --> 00:20:14,852
And so we ended up letting them go.

170
00:20:15,630 --> 00:20:21,348
But even heavy AI users were not immune to fears of losing their jobs or falling behind.

171
00:20:21,838 --> 00:20:28,104
One operations manager said she woke at 3 a.m. fearing for her career, and asked Claude,

172
00:20:28,104 --> 00:20:34,116
How do I make sure I set myself apart so that AI can't do everything that I do?

173
00:20:34,990 --> 00:20:36,548
These cases were rare.

174
00:20:36,974 --> 00:20:49,984
Overall, people at five companies described pushing out employees who would not adopt AI, and only two had seen AI automation fully eliminate a position and lead to someone losing their job.

175
00:20:50,224 --> 00:21:03,652
By contrast, ten companies reported hiring more due to AI, primarily into AI implementation roles and to bolster IT support, and others had grown rapidly for a variety of business reasons.

176
00:21:04,398 --> 00:21:07,492
But layoffs weren't the only adverse employment effect.

177
00:21:08,014 --> 00:21:15,140
People at two thirds of the companies we spoke to reported reduced hiring due to AI, as one person put it.

178
00:21:15,950 --> 00:21:19,396
We may not be firing, but we're not hiring.

179
00:21:20,174 --> 00:21:28,036
A third of these hiring declines were due to companies actively shrinking functions through attrition without backfilling the roles.

180
00:21:28,718 --> 00:21:36,356
But most were the result of companies foregoing hiring that in the absence of AI they would have needed to scale their businesses.

181
00:21:36,974 --> 00:21:43,044
A finding consistent with past work suggesting that AI-native startups employ fewer workers.

182
00:21:43,406 --> 00:21:47,184
As an engineering leader at a health management company put it,

183
00:21:47,184 --> 00:21:51,492
we've added a lot of capacity by buying tokens instead of hiring people.

184
00:21:52,206 --> 00:22:07,024
Beyond terminations and reduced hiring, nine companies described cutting contracted software developers, customer service representatives, legal counsel, and a range of back office functions, many of which they had already offshored.

185
00:22:07,214 --> 00:22:21,028
These outsourced jobs, which are based on modular tasks, self-contained pieces of work that can be handed off separately, sometimes without high expectations for quality, have been particularly vulnerable to replacement.

186
00:22:21,744 --> 00:22:33,024
An executive at a company that worked with an offshore design firm said, we've since fired them because now we can get better quality things faster and cheaper with AI.

187
00:22:33,264 --> 00:22:38,692
A finance company downsized well over 100 offshore data and developer contractors.

188
00:22:39,374 --> 00:22:47,876
An operations leader at a software company explained that with AI-driven contract reviews, their legal bill has just completely disappeared.

189
00:22:48,366 --> 00:22:52,836
These reductions were often the result of top-down workflow automation.

190
00:22:53,550 --> 00:23:02,916
For example, one financial technology company adopted AI customer service agents and cut its offshore customer service contracts.

191
00:23:03,150 --> 00:23:21,060
Instead of managing human customer service representatives and fielding escalated calls themselves, customer service managers now review transcripts of conversations with the AI agents and edit the agent's prompts, knowledge graphs, and general operating procedures.

192
00:23:21,664 --> 00:23:25,892
One company built an agent as part of a document review workflow.

193
00:23:26,126 --> 00:23:33,316
The agent reads submissions against the company's approval policies and passes a structured analysis to human reviewers.

194
00:23:34,094 --> 00:23:41,184
Deploying the agent led the company to cut the number of reviewers it expected to hire for the coming year by more than 90%.

195
00:23:41,998 --> 00:23:55,428
These agentic automations, in which AI agents are given instructions and then operate largely autonomously, are more advanced than the chatbots that still define many employees' experiences with AI.

196
00:23:55,886 --> 00:24:04,704
AI-native startups take these automated workflows to the extreme, both in the products they offer and in how they organize their own work.

197
00:24:05,006 --> 00:24:17,700
One chief operating officer walked us through his one-stop agentic portal for operations, noting that 90% of his operations budget was spent on AI tokens and 10% on headcount.

198
00:24:18,350 --> 00:24:20,104
He said of his approach

199
00:24:20,104 --> 00:24:23,172
Look, don't hire anybody on the back office.

200
00:24:23,406 --> 00:24:24,644
I'm going to automate it.

201
00:24:25,422 --> 00:24:33,796
One AI startup founder told us bluntly that their company's pitch was to replace the back office workers their customers currently employed.

202
00:24:49,134 --> 00:24:59,504
Another startup founder estimated that their company spent as much on internal AI use as on labor, and that it would need three times as many employees without AI.

203
00:24:59,744 --> 00:25:08,612
Overall, the six startups that gave us an estimate said they would need, on average, four times as many employees without AI.

204
00:25:09,006 --> 00:25:13,892
Another top-down deployment involves using AI to monitor employees more closely.

205
00:25:14,350 --> 00:25:28,228
Under the Anthropic Usage Policy, employee monitoring and its scope must be disclosed to employees, and the monitoring must be limited to employers' systems for security, safety, logistics, or compliance purposes.

206
00:25:28,622 --> 00:25:39,460
The policy further defines employment decisions, like evaluating an employee's performance as high risk and requires a human in the loop and disclosure to employees.

207
00:25:40,302 --> 00:25:47,504
The workplace monitoring we heard about appeared to be compliant with these restrictions and did not always involve Claude.

208
00:25:48,014 --> 00:25:50,532
One engineering executive explained.

209
00:25:51,342 --> 00:25:53,988
You want to make your work legible to your manager.

210
00:25:54,478 --> 00:25:59,876
But nowadays, I think you want to make your work legible to the agents your manager uses.

211
00:26:00,654 --> 00:26:16,576
This executive and others we spoke to were ambivalent about the implications of these capabilities, seeing both the benefits of more accurate performance feedback and the risks of workplace surveillance, a panopticon effect, as one executive put it.

212
00:26:16,816 --> 00:26:26,852
An operations supervisor said he started each day by reviewing an AI report on the throughput and error rate of each member of his 12-person document extraction team.

213
00:26:27,694 --> 00:26:30,948
If somehow there was a lag, then I can question back to them.

214
00:26:31,500 --> 00:26:33,156
OK, what happened this hour?

215
00:26:33,934 --> 00:26:40,420
Another operations director set up an AI-based assessment process for the firm's customer service representatives.

216
00:26:41,230 --> 00:26:51,140
An agent codes their transcripts according to dozens of performance dimensions, then routes the results to a dashboard monitored by the customer service representatives' managers.

217
00:26:51,790 --> 00:27:03,012
Top-down deployments like these shift power from employees to executives and owners, but they can also be brittle because they can quickly become out of date or obsolete.

218
00:27:03,502 --> 00:27:14,596
For example, the dozens of performance dimensions that customer service representatives were rated on are scored by a two-year-old model and are not taken seriously by managers.

219
00:27:14,926 --> 00:27:24,580
Similarly, a law firm built a series of automations for document management, but ended up with a number of low-quality zombie apps that were hardly used.

220
00:27:24,976 --> 00:27:37,216
One engineering leader described a chatbot their company had built for customer service workers, an early experiment built on an open source chat interface, as super basic, just chat and answer.

221
00:27:37,456 --> 00:27:43,780
They said the system was difficult to update and maintain, and that we haven't spent any more time or money on it.

222
00:27:44,238 --> 00:27:54,916
These systems are largely built by outside vendors rather than developed directly by internal users, and are marketed as targeted solutions for a specific industry and function.

223
00:27:55,662 --> 00:28:03,300
In interview after interview, companies described how improvements in model capabilities rendered these products outdated.

224
00:28:04,046 --> 00:28:06,776
As one employee said of one such system,

225
00:28:06,776 --> 00:28:09,188
it'll be obsolete in three months.

226
00:28:09,902 --> 00:28:17,732
One executive despaired of the upfront investments needed to build top-down workflows as models rapidly improved.

227
00:28:18,478 --> 00:28:22,084
It's almost like we're trying to throw darts at a spinning dartboard.

228
00:28:22,830 --> 00:28:31,044
Similar problems emerge in even the most ambitious automation attempts, those targeting knowledge work that requires deep expertise.

229
00:28:31,856 --> 00:28:40,132
Standing up effective workflows in these areas typically requires experts to design the agents' prompts and evaluate their outputs.

230
00:28:40,896 --> 00:28:47,012
But this means experts are effectively being asked to build and maintain the very systems that could replace them.

231
00:28:47,296 --> 00:28:53,508
One software engineer, who we'll call Ellen, described her first encounter with automated coding.

232
00:28:54,286 --> 00:28:56,260
I don't think that my job is secure.

233
00:28:56,878 --> 00:28:59,300
Software engineers are going to be obsolete.

234
00:29:00,046 --> 00:29:02,564
Another employee put it even more bluntly.

235
00:29:03,950 --> 00:29:08,004
If I'm open to this, I'm essentially building my own coffin.

236
00:29:08,750 --> 00:29:13,156
Managers also end up facing understandable resistance from employees.

237
00:29:13,710 --> 00:29:17,636
Recall the company that used AI to automate an approval workflow.

238
00:29:18,190 --> 00:29:23,696
It intended to double the output of expert reviewers and reduce hiring by more than 90%.

239
00:29:24,590 --> 00:29:28,132
The human reviewers balked, as one executive put it.

240
00:29:29,070 --> 00:29:35,972
Just because a reviewer can now do double the amount of cases, how do you incentivize them to do double the amount of cases?

241
00:29:36,718 --> 00:29:41,060
The company considered offering gift cards to get reviewers to comply.

242
00:29:41,710 --> 00:29:51,844
At the time of our interview, the executive noted that while some reviewers were down to two minutes of review per submission, there's some that are hanging out in the 10 plus minutes.

243
00:29:52,398 --> 00:29:57,444
Likewise recalled the senior developer who'd automated one law firm's mailroom.

244
00:29:58,190 --> 00:30:09,636
Fresh off that success, he told us, his COO put him on a more ambitious project, using AI to check for conflicts of interest among the firm's prospective clients.

245
00:30:10,416 --> 00:30:19,216
Under the existing process, conflict staff would run a search, print out any hits, and pass the buck right on to the attorney.

246
00:30:19,456 --> 00:30:30,084
In under a week, he built a workflow that let attorneys request a check from an AI agent in plain language, which then routed the request through conflict checking software.

247
00:30:30,862 --> 00:30:42,820
But the new workflow left it to the conflict staff to work through each potential conflict, and would often surface the types of complex and difficult conflicts that had previously been passed to the attorneys.

248
00:30:43,246 --> 00:30:50,340
Faced with this new work, the staff pushed back, the agent was shelved, and the firm returned to its old process.

249
00:30:50,926 --> 00:30:58,020
Top-down AI deployment is already changing hiring and contracting patterns by automating whole workflows.

250
00:30:58,256 --> 00:31:03,652
It is also prompting resistance from experts and raising fears of workplace surveillance.

251
00:31:04,336 --> 00:31:08,612
That resistance can result in abandoned or fragile automations.

252
00:31:10,254 --> 00:31:11,972
Bottom-up experimentation.

253
00:31:12,590 --> 00:31:18,564
For many companies, the problems of layoffs or the AI Panopticon haven't really arrived.

254
00:31:18,896 --> 00:31:24,740
Recall Mark, the paralegal, who worried for his job after being called in to meet his bosses.

255
00:31:25,326 --> 00:31:27,492
The meeting was not the one he expected.

256
00:31:27,982 --> 00:31:37,056
Mark explained to us that at the meeting, the partners noted that his demand letters were better than others and asked him how he'd prompted AI so effectively.

257
00:31:37,326 --> 00:31:40,772
Mark said he'd felt hesitant about sharing his workflows.

258
00:31:41,358 --> 00:31:43,176
He remembered telling them,

259
00:31:43,176 --> 00:31:44,964
That's my secret sauce.

260
00:31:45,486 --> 00:31:51,780
I have my own workflow, my own process, and respectfully, please do not take this the wrong way.

261
00:31:52,174 --> 00:31:54,052
I do not want to share with anyone.

262
00:31:54,798 --> 00:32:05,316
This pattern of a few individual contributors thriving with AI came up in many of our interviews, particularly for companies doing bottom-up deployments.

263
00:32:05,966 --> 00:32:14,340
Non-technical knowledge workers with substantial expertise in a given domain often derived the highest value from such deployments.

264
00:32:14,798 --> 00:32:22,692
This is in line with prior research on Claude code usage, which found that non-technical experts had the highest task success rates.

265
00:32:23,136 --> 00:32:33,216
One accountant described VIBECoding an app that audits whether the 401k payments employers process actually end up in the investment fund they are intended for.

266
00:32:33,456 --> 00:32:42,372
This audit process requires matching payment records across dozens of different systems and providers, and previously took accountants 80 hours.

267
00:32:43,380 --> 00:32:45,140
I was able to show that to our team.

268
00:32:45,740 --> 00:32:48,660
They were like, wow, this is super cool.

269
00:32:48,660 --> 00:32:51,190
The accountant said,

270
00:32:51,190 --> 00:32:54,660
They looked at me and they were like, You built this?

271
00:32:55,214 --> 00:32:57,100
And I was like, sort of.

272
00:32:57,646 --> 00:32:59,760
I told Claude what I needed to do.

273
00:33:00,660 --> 00:33:04,932
In these cases, coding tools clearly empower domain experts.

274
00:33:05,760 --> 00:33:11,376
A former airport operations manager told us about building a vehicle tracking app, saying,

275
00:33:11,376 --> 00:33:14,436
I was wielding AI like Excalibur.

276
00:33:15,214 --> 00:33:23,588
Quality assurance supervisors at a chemical plant built an app to monitor filtration rates despite having no coding experience.

277
00:33:24,558 --> 00:33:27,332
They know they're using Python because Claude told them.

278
00:33:28,078 --> 00:33:35,300
However, even the most dramatic success stories were often limited to changes that could be made from the bottom up.

279
00:33:35,982 --> 00:33:39,012
An employee at a manufacturing company explained.

280
00:33:39,822 --> 00:33:48,516
We're getting just these pockets of really cool stuff that could be great for our organization, but not necessarily a way to enable everyone to use that.

281
00:33:49,326 --> 00:33:56,996
Swapping out a single software tool or building a new personal productivity app can be done by ambitious individual contributors.

282
00:33:57,678 --> 00:34:06,628
One investment manager at a real estate firm developed a deal tracker that replaced a third-party product that cost $10,000 per year.

283
00:34:07,278 --> 00:34:13,668
Another created a legacy Windows XP app that would have cost $250,000 to replace.

284
00:34:14,222 --> 00:34:27,172
While many of these homegrown software replacements seem like notable upgrades at first, they still require review and ongoing maintenance, which means they can result in relatively small savings overall.

285
00:34:28,014 --> 00:34:33,792
How customized, and therefore how complex, does a deal tracker really need to be?

286
00:34:34,030 --> 00:34:42,500
An operations director at a large law firm considered building out dashboards to monitor their recruitment pipeline, but realized.

287
00:34:43,246 --> 00:34:44,388
That's small beans.

288
00:34:44,878 --> 00:34:48,388
There are plenty of SaaS products out there that can help you make dashboards.

289
00:34:49,070 --> 00:34:54,755
What is it, $30 a month, $50 a month, $100 a month, even?

290
00:34:55,373 --> 00:34:57,764
So we can save that money by vibe coding.

291
00:34:58,286 --> 00:35:05,380
But the maintenance cost obviously goes up as well, and we have to get our talent manager to start maintaining a vibe coded dashboard.

292
00:35:06,190 --> 00:35:17,232
Likewise, an engineer at a government services firm considered using AI to customize their software, but decided that even if it helped them win a few contracts,

293
00:35:17,232 --> 00:35:21,092
we don't think it's super sustainable from the maintenance and support side still.

294
00:35:21,582 --> 00:35:23,428
It just requires a lot of staffing.

295
00:35:24,206 --> 00:35:37,956
Figure three summarizes the 140 pieces of software that interviewees described as being built with AI, broken down by the software's purpose and the role of the person who worked with AI to build it.

296
00:35:38,638 --> 00:35:50,308
It shows that product features and technical infrastructure remain solidly within the purview of technical employees, while non-technical employees focus on internal apps and dashboards.

297
00:35:50,798 --> 00:35:59,204
This implies that the bulk of software built by non-developers still requires support or infrastructure from technical employees.

298
00:36:00,238 --> 00:36:00,592
Image.

299
00:36:01,134 --> 00:36:09,284
A bubble chart titled Software Built with AI, with job roles as rows and the software's purpose as columns.

300
00:36:10,030 --> 00:36:25,604
Engineers dominate products and client work with 38 pieces and technical infrastructure with 18, while non-technical roles, executives above all, cluster in internal apps, dashboards, and agents.

301
00:36:26,414 --> 00:36:27,300
Figure 3.

302
00:36:27,662 --> 00:36:32,548
Software built with AI across technical and non-technical functions.

303
00:36:33,102 --> 00:36:37,124
Each piece of AI-generated software discussed in our interviews.

304
00:36:37,518 --> 00:36:40,388
Rows indicate the roles of the software's authors.

305
00:36:40,974 --> 00:36:43,172
Columns indicate the software's purpose.

306
00:36:44,046 --> 00:36:48,100
Bubble sizes reflect the number of examples in each role by purpose.

307
00:36:48,526 --> 00:36:56,516
An automated coder classified both the software authors and the types of software according to the descriptions in the interview transcripts.

308
00:36:57,294 --> 00:37:11,556
Beyond maintenance, these one-off, bottom-up coding projects can create substantially more work elsewhere in the organization, either in the last mile of production or through uneven acceleration across different tasks.

309
00:37:11,952 --> 00:37:21,392
The 401k audit app prototype mentioned above had to be implemented by a third-party vendor and was still not in active use months later.

310
00:37:21,646 --> 00:37:31,192
An engineer at an equipment manufacturer realized that AI could translate the text on their product surfaces into 10 different languages, but

311
00:37:31,192 --> 00:37:34,948
we don't have anybody that can answer the phone in those languages.

312
00:37:35,502 --> 00:37:37,732
So we ended up turning that feature off.

313
00:37:38,126 --> 00:37:39,364
We didn't even ship it.

314
00:37:40,142 --> 00:37:44,644
As coding accelerates relative to other functions, it hits bottlenecks.

315
00:37:45,998 --> 00:37:50,596
Without the proper scaffolding in place, that bottleneck is immediate.

316
00:37:51,342 --> 00:37:56,964
These uneven changes in productivity can also run into organizational politics.

317
00:37:57,486 --> 00:37:59,328
As one manager put it,

318
00:37:59,328 --> 00:38:04,132
in our world, it's like Billy in sales doesn't trust Sally in marketing.

319
00:38:04,878 --> 00:38:12,676
Exacerbating these conflicts is the fact that the uneven adoption of AI can impose costs on non-adopters.

320
00:38:13,230 --> 00:38:21,060
Power users lob clawed grenades and shoot clawed cannons at the rest of the organization, while AI skeptics respond.

321
00:38:21,870 --> 00:38:25,060
Guys, I'm not reading your AI email.

322
00:38:25,806 --> 00:38:35,684
In one case, an AI agent put together a set of profit and loss projections that a chief investment officer realized were based on outdated assumptions.

323
00:38:36,270 --> 00:38:39,128
As one frustrated executive put it,

324
00:38:39,128 --> 00:38:43,460
a mediocre engineer with an AI is a menace to society.

325
00:38:44,206 --> 00:38:49,988
Figure 4 summarizes the bottlenecks to deriving value from AI discussed across the interviews.

326
00:38:50,638 --> 00:38:53,732
Many involve the uneven acceleration of tasks.

327
00:38:54,254 --> 00:39:01,124
The difficulty of reviewing AI outputs came up again and again, as their volume swamped scarce human attention.

328
00:39:01,966 --> 00:39:09,252
In one incident, an agent added an errant S to an API endpoint and broke a carefully planned feature release.

329
00:39:09,614 --> 00:39:14,564
In other cases, when coding accelerated, other functions could not keep up.

330
00:39:15,054 --> 00:39:22,244
Uneven employee adoption was also common, as the enthusiasm of power users failed to rub off on their peers.

331
00:39:22,926 --> 00:39:30,564
Companies also often noted that they lacked the necessary context and infrastructure to support AI-generated software.

332
00:39:31,246 --> 00:39:36,848
By contrast, customer skepticism and regulation were rarely cited as bottlenecks.

333
00:39:37,230 --> 00:39:43,204
The bulk of bottlenecks appear to emerge from the process of adopting AI within the organization itself.

334
00:39:44,014 --> 00:39:44,612
Image.

335
00:39:45,038 --> 00:39:50,436
A bar chart titled Bottlenecks to Getting Value from AI, grouped by theme.

336
00:39:51,118 --> 00:39:56,068
The longest bars are Too Much Output for People to Check, cited 49 times.

337
00:39:56,654 --> 00:40:03,524
Staff resisting AI use, 43, and required expert review and testing, 42.

338
00:40:04,174 --> 00:40:10,372
Customers wary of AI, at 13, and regulation, at 4, sit near the bottom.

339
00:40:11,278 --> 00:40:14,884
Figure 4, bottlenecks to getting value from AI.

340
00:40:15,470 --> 00:40:22,180
All bottlenecks to AI adoption, diffusion, and value realization mentioned in our interviews.

341
00:40:22,528 --> 00:40:28,804
An automated coder searched interviews for any discussion of barriers and blockers to implementing AI.

342
00:40:29,326 --> 00:40:40,708
These examples evoke Paul David's mill buildings, retrofitted with electric engines, a classic account of why productivity growth may lag technological implementation.

343
00:40:41,390 --> 00:40:51,940
Individual contributors can only revamp processes and decisions within their own small corners of an organization, while the broader division of labor remains in place.

344
00:40:52,622 --> 00:40:56,448
As one Hotel services operations Director put it,

345
00:40:56,448 --> 00:40:59,844
the individual doesn't have the authority to change the work.

346
00:41:00,174 --> 00:41:02,276
They can only apply it in their own area.

347
00:41:02,990 --> 00:41:08,772
The head of AI at an investment firm voiced similar frustration with the bottom-up projects he had seen.

348
00:41:09,614 --> 00:41:15,528
How can we upskill and tear down our workflows and rebuild like we were starting the company again

349
00:41:15,528 --> 00:41:15,812
now?

350
00:41:16,334 --> 00:41:17,088
Not Claude

351
00:41:17,088 --> 00:41:19,328
write the email, but why are you even

352
00:41:19,328 --> 00:41:20,356
getting the email?

353
00:41:21,134 --> 00:41:26,404
One manager described the challenge of using AI agents to automate production planning.

354
00:41:26,958 --> 00:41:36,068
Setting output targets required input from a half dozen functions, spanning procurement, logistics, manufacturing, sales, and planning.

355
00:41:37,134 --> 00:41:40,848
They've done a poor job at defining who has accountability

356
00:41:40,848 --> 00:41:42,916
and authority to make decisions.

357
00:41:43,700 --> 00:41:47,240
The manager noted that only executive pressure could force change.

358
00:41:48,814 --> 00:41:53,476
Getting them to imagine doing their job differently can't come from them.

359
00:41:53,902 --> 00:41:55,844
It has to come top-down.

360
00:41:57,166 --> 00:41:59,204
Reorganizing around AI.

361
00:41:59,726 --> 00:42:01,216
What is the alternative?

362
00:42:01,646 --> 00:42:11,332
Can AI deployment avoid both brittle automation of low-value tasks and the bottleneck of hobbyist projects the organization can't absorb?

363
00:42:12,526 --> 00:42:18,724
Mark, the paralegal, ultimately agreed to share his approach with the partners and the rest of his firm.

364
00:42:19,854 --> 00:42:26,820
He had effectively automated the most relaxed part of his job, firing off AI-written demand letters.

365
00:42:27,214 --> 00:42:33,060
But he gained a new responsibility, supporting his firm's AI transition and enablement.

366
00:42:33,518 --> 00:42:43,936
Some companies are experimenting with complementary organizational changes in response to AI, rather than just automating existing workflows or handing out AI tools.

367
00:42:44,238 --> 00:42:59,652
Here we look at three ways companies have pursued these organizational changes: broadening roles to reduce coordination costs, redeploying employees toward bottleneck tasks, and building common infrastructure to support non-technical builders.

368
00:43:02,958 --> 00:43:11,652
When firms broaden employees' roles, individuals become responsible for an entire workflow rather than one narrow type of expertise.

369
00:43:12,096 --> 00:43:18,820
Engineers, for example, might start working across product, design, and even customer engagement.

370
00:43:19,376 --> 00:43:28,336
One healthcare startup asked all of its engineers, not just its business people, to speak regularly to medical professionals who use their product.

371
00:43:28,576 --> 00:43:40,868
Having a single employee or small team own an entire product or customer relationship limits the organizational friction and potential vetoes that can otherwise block AI acceleration.

372
00:43:41,998 --> 00:43:44,456
As one engineering leader put it,

373
00:43:44,456 --> 00:43:47,652
two people plus Claude is the most amazing thing.

374
00:43:48,206 --> 00:43:50,436
If two people agree on something, just ship it.

375
00:43:51,214 --> 00:44:06,980
As production, not just of code, but also of legal research, data analysis, financial modeling, and other aspects of knowledge work speeds up, agreeing on what to build across the organization can become slow and costly.

376
00:44:07,726 --> 00:44:15,856
But the more a single individual or a small team controls an entire workflow, the less these coordination costs appear.

377
00:44:16,110 --> 00:44:27,780
One executive described rethinking their lead sourcing, account executive, and customer success roles, merging them into a single role that handles the whole customer lifecycle.

378
00:44:28,558 --> 00:44:39,136
AI tools made keeping up with the details of each step in the deal process easier and made the context loss associated with handing a task off to a colleague more costly.

379
00:44:39,374 --> 00:44:48,580
One startup founder described exclusively employing individual contributors and giving each of them ownership over a distinct part of the product and business.

380
00:44:49,550 --> 00:45:00,068
At another company, a manager explained that we essentially collapsed designer, product manager, engineer and QA, and even infrastructure into a singular role.

381
00:45:00,270 --> 00:45:05,764
Another engineering leader aimed at more full-body contact of engineers with customers.

382
00:45:06,176 --> 00:45:12,196
Figure 5 shows examples of roles broadening from specific functions to whole workflows.

383
00:45:13,102 --> 00:45:13,456
Image.

384
00:45:13,960 --> 00:45:17,860
a diagram titled broadening roles with AI adoption.

385
00:45:18,542 --> 00:45:30,120
With 10 rows of separate jobs, each pointing to one job, product manager, designer, front end, back end, and QA become one end-to-end builder.

386
00:45:30,896 --> 00:45:38,084
Managers and individual contributors become twelve individual contributors, each directing agents.

387
00:45:38,926 --> 00:45:39,840
Figure 5.

388
00:45:40,080 --> 00:45:41,892
Examples of role broadening.

389
00:45:42,478 --> 00:45:46,212
Examples of role broadening across functions from our interviews.

390
00:45:46,862 --> 00:45:54,596
Some instances are changes to existing roles, others are new roles that combine tasks from multiple traditional functions.

391
00:45:55,310 --> 00:46:03,620
Another benefit of role broadening is that even as some parts of a job are automated, experts have less reason to fear job loss.

392
00:46:04,238 --> 00:46:12,260
At current model capabilities, agentic workflows still typically need active expert engagement and input to develop.

393
00:46:12,878 --> 00:46:18,480
They also require ongoing maintenance as models improve and workflows change.

394
00:46:18,734 --> 00:46:27,140
For example, one engineer automated a system to respond to production alert tickets, which had previously been a substantial part of his job.

395
00:46:27,520 --> 00:46:43,172
For two months after deploying the system, the engineer read transcripts of every conversation with the AI agent and tweaked the prompts, repaired broken integrations, supplied missing vendor documentation, and approved agents' authorization requests.

396
00:46:43,758 --> 00:46:51,620
Six months in, with the system working well, he still sampled transcripts every two weeks to update and maintain the system.

397
00:46:52,206 --> 00:46:53,924
He also kept building on it.

398
00:46:54,350 --> 00:46:57,636
The alert agent became a company-wide Slack agent.

399
00:46:58,350 --> 00:47:00,356
The company basically runs on it now.

400
00:47:01,134 --> 00:47:06,436
And then an agent that keeps skills and context up to date on everyone's laptop.

401
00:47:07,214 --> 00:47:14,116
Some companies have also found creative ways to transfer employees to new roles and build their expertise.

402
00:47:14,638 --> 00:47:30,212
A financial services firm, for example, shifted its internship program away from its immediate business needs toward helping interns develop domain expertise, such that now the sole purpose is to build expertise and domain knowledge for that person.

403
00:47:31,662 --> 00:47:34,820
Redeploying employees toward bottleneck tasks.

404
00:47:35,406 --> 00:47:42,480
Some companies respond to AI-enabled acceleration by shifting employees toward the tasks that are still bottlenecks.

405
00:47:43,022 --> 00:47:48,996
If employees can chase down bottlenecks, they can better complement the capabilities of AI models.

406
00:47:49,454 --> 00:47:55,236
Recall Ellen, the software engineer who was initially afraid she would lose her job to coding agents.

407
00:47:55,694 --> 00:48:02,084
She eventually accepted that the entire job has switched right from writing code to making decisions.

408
00:48:02,800 --> 00:48:12,420
At a consulting company, consultants helped automate the process of reconciling best practices and standards documents that conflicted with one another.

409
00:48:12,880 --> 00:48:19,172
The consultants then shifted to more person-to-person consulting work instead of sifting through documents.

410
00:48:19,566 --> 00:48:31,204
Likewise, an engineering director suggested that their team spend more time talking to your PM product manager and helping your PM raise relevant questions against the user journey.

411
00:48:31,680 --> 00:48:37,508
An operations leader said automation gave them more time for the process change and the buy-in.

412
00:48:38,062 --> 00:48:41,840
Honestly, that is the biggest battle, that process change.

413
00:48:42,062 --> 00:48:49,668
In these cases, the work shifted toward the interpersonal and strategic tasks that limit models' productivity.

414
00:48:50,094 --> 00:48:59,140
As another example, project managers at a construction company contributed to an agentic workflow for checking in across construction sites.

415
00:48:59,918 --> 00:49:09,092
Previously, project managers would call crew members at different sites every morning, put notes together, and write up a daily report for customers.

416
00:49:09,966 --> 00:49:15,280
Now, an AI agent pings crew members who talk to the robot instead.

417
00:49:15,534 --> 00:49:23,140
The agent synthesizes the responses, reviews previous reports, drafts a new one, and sends it to the customer.

418
00:49:23,886 --> 00:49:29,092
Project managers said they got their mornings back to talk to customers about more pressing needs.

419
00:49:30,094 --> 00:49:32,240
An excellent customer experience.

420
00:49:32,494 --> 00:49:33,508
That's number one.

421
00:49:34,254 --> 00:49:44,836
Similarly, one CTO shifted his product manager's focus to aspects of the role that require more taste or the ability to identify product market fit.

422
00:49:45,486 --> 00:49:47,360
As the CTO put it,

423
00:49:47,360 --> 00:49:50,052
Claude is a terrible product manager.

424
00:49:50,606 --> 00:49:51,920
So it's a lot of taste

425
00:49:51,920 --> 00:49:53,796
deciding what features are important.

426
00:49:54,702 --> 00:50:00,900
For engineers, code review became a critical bottleneck, so many of them have worked on solving it.

427
00:50:01,582 --> 00:50:02,880
One engineer explained.

428
00:50:03,950 --> 00:50:08,420
You find a bottleneck, find out how to tackle that, have someone own that.

429
00:50:08,846 --> 00:50:10,404
Code's no longer the problem.

430
00:50:10,734 --> 00:50:13,156
Let's put time in user experience testing.

431
00:50:13,454 --> 00:50:15,492
Let's put time in requirements gathering.

432
00:50:16,302 --> 00:50:23,652
Some companies worked on automating code review, while others expanded the share of time engineers spent reviewing code.

433
00:50:24,048 --> 00:50:31,748
One company abandoned formal code review altogether, but had developers work side by side with Claude to assess code.

434
00:50:32,238 --> 00:50:45,540
The pressure to flexibly shift toward bottlenecks is especially strong with AI, because the models improve too fast for any fixed organizational plan to keep pace, as one HR manager put it.

435
00:50:46,380 --> 00:50:51,020
You wait three months and something new you didn't think AI could do is now possible.

436
00:50:51,726 --> 00:50:56,100
And you're like, well, okay, let me revise my whole plan.

437
00:50:56,974 --> 00:50:58,528
A CTO said,

438
00:50:58,528 --> 00:51:04,644
If the world could just pause for a minute and let us get some harnesses in place, that would be great.

439
00:51:05,358 --> 00:51:11,876
AI adoption benefits from scaffolding that makes role changes easier as the bottlenecks keep moving.

440
00:51:12,462 --> 00:51:16,836
Figure six shows what these role changes look like across a variety of jobs.

441
00:51:17,486 --> 00:51:28,740
As software development becomes the purview of a whole gamut of non-technical functions, engineers broaden their scope across design, product management, and even core business functions.

442
00:51:29,134 --> 00:51:31,972
At the same time, new tasks emerge.

443
00:51:32,366 --> 00:51:36,900
Certified public accountants grade an AI finance agent's outputs.

444
00:51:37,358 --> 00:51:40,644
Marketing executives edit branding skill prompts.

445
00:51:41,230 --> 00:51:44,964
IT decides which non-developer apps to put into production.

446
00:51:45,742 --> 00:51:50,276
A client experience manager builds synthetic customers to pitch ideas to.

447
00:51:50,680 --> 00:52:01,252
Production planners, accustomed to poring over spreadsheets, become data labelers, providing qualitative context for AI agents to better understand the numbers.

448
00:52:02,240 --> 00:52:03,400
You should be a journalist.

449
00:52:04,302 --> 00:52:06,920
You should not think about competing with AI.

450
00:52:07,822 --> 00:52:18,852
Representatives at the largest firms we interviewed said they were systematically inventorying employees' skills and tracking shifts in their labor needs to formalize these changes.

451
00:52:19,726 --> 00:52:29,088
At one HR support firm, an agentic workflow automated the work of a team that had assessed clients' employees for benefits eligibility.

452
00:52:29,358 --> 00:52:39,268
The team pivoted to client-facing work, answering calls and providing high-touch customer support, which the company had not previously had the resources to do.

453
00:52:39,950 --> 00:52:51,780
At a construction company, a contract administrator helped automate their own data gathering work and was redeployed to integrate AI across the company and maintain agentic workflows.

454
00:52:51,982 --> 00:52:56,388
Redeployments like these can keep automation from leading to layoffs.

455
00:52:57,198 --> 00:52:57,568
Image.

456
00:52:58,158 --> 00:53:05,764
A flow diagram titled Task Reallocation, linking who did each task before AI adoption to who does it now.

457
00:53:06,382 --> 00:53:16,004
The largest source is new work nobody did before, 68 tasks, and the largest destination is nobody, because it is automated, 37.

458
00:53:16,878 --> 00:53:21,732
Engineering tasks fan out to business functions, product managers and designers.

459
00:53:22,606 --> 00:53:23,488
Figure 6.

460
00:53:23,854 --> 00:53:26,148
Task reallocation across jobs.

461
00:53:26,638 --> 00:53:32,260
Cataloging task reallocations based on every description of a task change from our interviews.

462
00:53:32,608 --> 00:53:39,844
Each change is coded according to the person who previously performed it and the person who performed it after AI adoption.

463
00:53:40,558 --> 00:53:43,108
Red represents software engineering roles.

464
00:53:43,662 --> 00:53:45,892
Blue represents other knowledge work.

465
00:53:51,118 --> 00:53:53,060
Building common infrastructure.

466
00:53:53,550 --> 00:54:05,700
Several companies have set up common infrastructure that allows non technical people to build apps that do not expose security vulnerabilities or require substantial maintenance by engineers.

467
00:54:06,670 --> 00:54:12,804
Every single employee now is a developer, creating additional attack surface every day.

468
00:54:13,582 --> 00:54:15,972
A data security executive told us.

469
00:54:16,654 --> 00:54:26,980
The head of engineering at a real estate company said they went from having a team of five developers to a team of dozens of developers that all want to release software.

470
00:54:27,438 --> 00:54:39,012
Perhaps the most important shared infrastructure is company data, which provides context both for increasingly autonomous agents and for employees across functions.

471
00:54:39,694 --> 00:54:48,676
With AI, an executive can query business data at the moment of need rather than waiting for a regular report from data analysts.

472
00:54:49,198 --> 00:54:58,212
A trucking company built an agent accessible data warehouse, and managers across the company began creating their own analyses based on that data.

473
00:54:58,894 --> 00:55:01,316
They're absolutely addicted to it.

474
00:55:02,030 --> 00:55:03,684
A person at the company said.

475
00:55:04,280 --> 00:55:13,060
Instead of a business intelligence or data science team mediating every request for analysis, it would have ended up on the queue sometimes a year or two.

476
00:55:13,518 --> 00:55:18,116
A broad range of knowledge workers started running SQL queries themselves.

477
00:55:18,592 --> 00:55:24,996
At another company, a sales team leapt into data analysis, linking sales and finance data.

478
00:55:25,774 --> 00:55:27,108
Anybody can do it.

479
00:55:27,758 --> 00:55:29,892
And so now everybody does it.

480
00:55:30,638 --> 00:55:33,432
Without AI, one employee said,

481
00:55:33,432 --> 00:55:39,012
they probably would have never seen the data, or they would have never tried to do anything with it.

482
00:55:39,758 --> 00:55:49,580
And the trust and safety team at an online marketplace used their new data analytics tools to identify fraud patterns, as one team member explained.

483
00:55:50,510 --> 00:55:53,412
We can now go parse through all of our logs.

484
00:55:54,190 --> 00:56:02,852
These examples all depended on companies first pulling in and consolidating their data so that AI had the context it needed.

485
00:56:03,374 --> 00:56:14,820
This is consistent with prior research on enterprise use, which found that more complex tasks tend to require more context from users, measured in input tokens.

486
00:56:15,502 --> 00:56:22,532
Making data available to the whole organization can shift power downward, as one executive put it.

487
00:56:23,360 --> 00:56:25,920
you have to trust the democratization of data.

488
00:56:26,798 --> 00:56:30,430
At one company, the reporting team initially protested,

489
00:56:30,430 --> 00:56:33,740
hey, we have to stop Claude from accessing data.

490
00:56:34,320 --> 00:56:37,140
People are reporting on things that we normally report on.

491
00:56:37,934 --> 00:56:43,940
But the AI advocates won out, and the work of the reporting and business intelligence teams changed.

492
00:56:44,432 --> 00:56:53,604
Rather than creating reports and building dashboards, the teams were able to offer advice and insight derived from more rapid and sophisticated analysis.

493
00:56:54,350 --> 00:56:58,884
For AI-native startups, a structured pool of data is critical.

494
00:56:59,374 --> 00:57:06,212
Teams transcribe and graph customer calls and pull meeting transcripts into Slack and other public surfaces.

495
00:57:07,150 --> 00:57:09,700
We just like transcribe everything.

496
00:57:10,446 --> 00:57:18,052
An AI lead at an investment firm described urging employees to move out of direct messages and work out loud.

497
00:57:18,606 --> 00:57:27,108
The same data and AI tooling can give executives more oversight of employees and give employees more leverage in the organization.

498
00:57:27,712 --> 00:57:37,700
Prior research has found that information technologies tend to push decision making down to employees, while communication technologies tend to centralize it.

499
00:57:37,902 --> 00:57:48,132
Our interview suggests that how organizations set up AI tools, such as who can access data, can tilt a given technology toward empowering employees.

500
00:57:49,710 --> 00:58:04,292
Conclusion The dozens of people we interviewed told us that AI has substantially automated a range of work at their companies, particularly in software engineering, customer service, and back office roles.

501
00:58:05,070 --> 00:58:15,268
But we also heard about a variety of bottlenecks that prevented productivity gains on particular tasks from translating into overall benefits for businesses.

502
00:58:16,160 --> 00:58:18,320
As one executive put it,

503
00:58:18,320 --> 00:58:23,428
the closer you are to the money, the harder it is for models to excel in that job.

504
00:58:24,206 --> 00:58:36,196
If prototyping is fast but deployment is not, or production is fast but marketing is slow, then AI tools deliver far less than model evaluations and capabilities would imply.

505
00:58:36,718 --> 00:58:40,580
The people working alongside AI face even deeper problems.

506
00:58:41,038 --> 00:58:46,148
In top-down deployments, automation and surveillance threaten to undermine employees.

507
00:58:46,638 --> 00:58:54,916
In bottom-up deployments, inconsistent velocity across the organization leads to inequality in performance between employees or teams.

508
00:58:55,566 --> 00:59:02,884
Companies that reorganized around AI addressed the challenges associated with both top-down and bottom-up approaches.

509
00:59:03,662 --> 00:59:11,332
Developers broadened their remit across the full software stack, then shifted toward product, design, and quality assurance.

510
00:59:12,142 --> 00:59:16,836
Non-technical employees found themselves needed for a whole range of novel tasks.

511
00:59:17,166 --> 00:59:22,980
The underlying approach was to shift people's jobs toward the tasks that AI can't yet accomplish.

512
00:59:23,694 --> 00:59:29,892
Broadening jobs also gave the people closest to the work the authority to redesign their workflows.

513
00:59:30,926 --> 00:59:39,652
And shared data and infrastructure helped knowledge workers collaborate with AI, extending the benefits of coding agents beyond engineers.

514
00:59:40,206 --> 00:59:45,412
Our interviews suggest a path that could benefit both employees and employers.

515
00:59:46,000 --> 00:59:52,996
Given current model capabilities, experts are still critical to building and reviewing AI workflows.

516
00:59:53,678 --> 00:59:59,652
Interpersonal and strategic tasks are currently the central bottlenecks where humans are needed.

517
01:00:00,142 --> 01:00:04,516
But the details of these roles will continue to change as models improve.

518
01:00:04,800 --> 01:00:16,740
Employers that develop systems to redeploy employees in new roles and broaden their scope may be best placed to take advantage of these intelligence gains without displacing employees.

519
01:00:17,198 --> 01:00:19,684
How can employers deliver on this potential?

520
01:00:20,240 --> 01:00:32,452
Our interviews suggest that some companies are already sharing the gains of AI deployments with their employees, using productivity gains to improve job quality and lighten tedious work.

521
01:00:32,880 --> 01:00:41,348
Sharing the gains in this way may also motivate employees to contribute their expertise to redesigning workflows for AI tools.

522
01:00:41,742 --> 01:00:47,652
Companies that commit to internal worker reallocation must plan ahead for where those workers could go.

523
01:00:48,080 --> 01:00:57,636
And they can give employees, including non-technical ones, broad access to the data and infrastructure they need to make the most of new AI tools.

524
01:00:58,446 --> 01:01:05,828
Finally, model developers like Anthropic must support these practices by offering training and help to business customers.

525
01:01:06,190 --> 01:01:10,000
We hope the findings in this report will inform those efforts.

526
01:01:10,958 --> 01:01:20,836
None of this is a guarantee against AI-induced job loss, and it will not replace a strong policy response in the event of substantial technological disruption.

527
01:01:21,422 --> 01:01:29,540
But the wide variation in how employers are deploying the same technology suggests they have some say in how AI changes work.

528
